---
import V9Layout from '~/layouts/V9Layout.astro';
import Content from '~/components/v9/content/Content.astro';
import { publicLinks } from '~/data/v9/preview-links';
import { fetchPosts } from '~/utils/blog';
import { getPermalink } from '~/utils/permalinks';
const post = (await fetchPosts()).find((post) => post.slug === 'blog/rethinking-hiring-metrics-signal-efficiency')!;

const shareUrl = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, 'https://www.tribera.ai').href;
---

<V9Layout
  title="Rethinking hiring metrics — tribera"
  description="Time-to-hire measures your speed. Interview-to-hire measures your aim. What 200,000 scored interviews taught us — and the thing we had backwards for two years."
  headerLinkOverrides={publicLinks}
  footerLinkOverrides={publicLinks}
  openGraphType="article"
  image="~/assets/images/blog/signal-efficiency.png"
>
  <Content kind="article">
    <div class="reading" aria-hidden="true"><span class="reading__f"></span></div>

    <header class="ahead band">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="ahead__meta">
          <time datetime={post.publishDate.toISOString()}
            >{
              post.publishDate.toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
                day: 'numeric',
                month: 'short',
                year: 'numeric',
                timeZone: 'UTC',
              })
            }</time
          ><i>·</i>
          {post.category && <a href={getPermalink(post.category.slug, 'category')}>{post.category.title}</a>}<i>·</i
          ><span>{post.readingTime} read</span>
        </p>
        <h1>Rethinking hiring metrics</h1>
        <p class="ahead__sub">
          What 200,000 interviews taught us about signal efficiency. And the thing we had backwards for two years.
        </p>
        <div class="ahead__by">
          <span class="ahead__ph" id="byPh" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="ahead__who"
            ><b>Akshata Chandavarkar</b>Founder &amp; CEO · twenty years of enterprise hiring</span
          >
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="band band--w">
      <div class="wrap">
        <article class="art">
          <p class="lede">
            Time-to-hire tells you how fast a process moved. It does not tell you whether the process was any good. This
            is about the number that does. And the thing we had backwards about it for two years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every talent team reports time-to-hire. Most report cost-per-hire and offer-acceptance alongside it. They
            are real numbers and they measure real things, and none of them will tell you whether the search you just
            ran was sound.
          </p>

          <p>Consider two roles that both closed in forty days.</p>

          <p>
            The first ran four interviews. One candidate, three rounds, an offer accepted. The hiring manager moved
            quickly because the shortlist was right and she could see why.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second ran twelve. Nine candidates, most of whom were never going to clear round two, and a manager who
            by week five had stopped trusting anything the pipeline sent her. She hired someone in the end. She also
            stopped opening the emails.
          </p>

          <figure class="fig">
            <svg class="fig__svg" viewBox="0 0 330 80" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"
              ><rect x="6" y="16" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#8E8A80" opacity="1"></rect><rect
                x="32"
                y="16"
                width="20"
                height="13"
                rx="3"
                fill="#8E8A80"
                opacity="1"></rect><rect x="58" y="16" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#8E8A80" opacity="1"
              ></rect><rect x="84" y="16" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#8E8A80" opacity="1"></rect><rect
                x="6"
                y="52"
                width="20"
                height="13"
                rx="3"
                fill="#C6C2B8"
                opacity="1"></rect><rect x="32" y="52" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="1"
              ></rect><rect x="58" y="52" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="1"></rect><rect
                x="84"
                y="52"
                width="20"
                height="13"
                rx="3"
                fill="#C6C2B8"
                opacity="1"></rect><rect x="110" y="52" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="1"
              ></rect><rect x="136" y="52" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="1"></rect><rect
                x="162"
                y="52"
                width="20"
                height="13"
                rx="3"
                fill="#C6C2B8"
                opacity="1"></rect><rect x="188" y="52" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="1"
              ></rect><rect x="214" y="52" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="1"></rect><rect
                x="240"
                y="52"
                width="20"
                height="13"
                rx="3"
                fill="#C6C2B8"
                opacity="1"></rect><rect x="266" y="52" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="1"
              ></rect><rect x="292" y="52" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="1"></rect><rect
                x="6"
                y="16"
                width="20"
                height="13"
                rx="3"
                fill="#DA0007"
                opacity="1"></rect><rect x="292" y="52" width="20" height="13" rx="3" fill="#DA0007" opacity="1"
              ></rect></svg
            >
            <figcaption>
              <b>Same forty days.</b> Four interviews above, twelve below. Time-to-hire cannot see the difference between
              these two searches, and the difference is the whole story.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2>The quietest number on the dashboard</h2>

          <p>
            Interview-to-hire ratio counts how many people your team had to meet before one of them was right. It is
            usually buried three tabs deep in an ATS report, and it is the most honest figure in there, because it is
            the only one that cannot be improved by working harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can cut time-to-hire by chasing people. You can cut cost-per-hire by using cheaper channels. The ratio
            does not move for either. It only moves when the shortlist gets better, which means it is not really a
            measure of your pipeline at all. It is a measure of how well the role was understood before anyone started
            looking.
          </p>

          <blockquote class="pull">
            Time-to-hire measures your speed.
            <em>Interview-to-hire measures your aim.</em>
          </blockquote>

          <h2>What we had backwards</h2>

          <p>
            For the first two years we read a poor ratio as a candidate-quality problem. If a team was meeting nine
            people to hire one, the obvious reading was that six of those nine should never have been on the list, and
            the fix was better screening.
          </p>

          <p>That was wrong, or at least it was the smaller half of the answer.</p>

          <p>
            When we went back through the mandates where the ratio was worst, the pattern was not weak candidates. In
            most of them the candidates were fine. What those searches had in common was that nobody had written down
            what good looked like before the search started. So the bar was assembled in the room, one interview at a
            time, and it moved.
          </p>

          <p>
            A manager who has not written the bar down does not have a fixed one. She has a comparison. Candidate four
            is judged against candidates one to three rather than against the job, and every new person shifts the frame
            slightly. That is why those searches feel like they are converging on something and never arrive.
          </p>

          <figure class="fig">
            <svg
              class="fig__svg"
              viewBox="0 0 300 106"
              fill="none"
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              aria-hidden="true"
              ><rect x="0" y="0" width="288.0" height="10" rx="5" fill="#DA0007" opacity="1"></rect><rect
                x="0"
                y="26"
                width="222.0"
                height="10"
                rx="5"
                fill="#8E8A80"
                opacity="1"></rect><rect x="0" y="52" width="183.0" height="10" rx="5" fill="#8E8A80" opacity="1"
              ></rect><rect x="0" y="78" width="69.0" height="10" rx="5" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="1"></rect></svg
            >
            <figcaption>
              Across the mandates where the ratio was worst, <b
                >the strongest correlation was whether the bar existed in writing before sourcing began</b
              >. Not candidate quality, which barely moved the number at all. Relative strength of association, our own
              mandates.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2>Where three comes from</h2>

          <p>
            Across the searches we run, three interviews per hire is roughly where a calibrated process settles. Not
            because three is a magic number, but because that is what happens when the bar is fixed in advance and
            everyone in the loop is reading the same one.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is worth saying plainly that this is not a target, and a team that games it will get nothing. Sending
            fewer people does not improve alignment; it just moves the failure later, into a hire nobody was sure about.
            The ratio is a symptom. Treating a symptom is how you end up with a number that looks healthy attached to a
            team that is guessing.
          </p>

          <h2>What to do with it on Monday</h2>

          <p>Three things, in the order of how little they cost.</p>

          <p>
            <b>Put the ratio next to time-to-hire in the same report.</b> Not instead of it — next to it. The two numbers
            together tell you something neither says alone, and it takes an afternoon to add the column.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Write the bar down before you source.</b> Four or five dimensions, weighted, agreed with the hiring manager
            in the intake conversation. It does not need to be elaborate. It needs to exist before the first CV, because a
            bar written after the first CV is a description of that CV.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Keep the reasoning, not just the outcome.</b> Most teams record who was rejected. Almost none record why, in
            a form anyone can read six months later. That record is what stops the eleventh search starting from the same
            blank page as the first.
          </p>

          <p>None of this makes hiring fast. It makes it legible, which is the thing that eventually makes it fast.</p>

          <p class="signoff">Belong. Build. Become.</p>
        </article>

        <div class="aft">
          <div class="atags">
            {
              post.tags?.map((tag) => (
                <a class="atag" href={getPermalink(tag.slug, 'tag')}>
                  {tag.title}
                </a>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <div class="ashare">
            <span>Share</span>
            <a
              href={`https://www.linkedin.com/sharing/share-offsite/?url=${encodeURIComponent(shareUrl)}`}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a
            >
            <a href={shareUrl} data-copy-article>Permalink</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap band" id="related">
      <div class="shead" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;text-align:center;justify-items:center;gap:8px">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--red" data-v9-reveal>Keep reading</p>
          <h2 class="h2" data-v9-reveal style="--d:60ms;max-width:none;margin-bottom:clamp(16px,2vw,24px)">
            Three more that came out of real mandates.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="dek" data-v9-reveal style="--d:100ms;max-width:none">
          Or go back to <a
            href="/blog"
            style="color:var(--red);
    text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(218,0,7,.35)"
            >everything we’ve published</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rel" data-v9-reveal style="--d:140ms">
        <a class="relc" href="/blog/designing-ai-for-high-stakes-technical-assessment"
          ><p class="relc__k">AI and assessment</p><p class="relc__t">Designing AI for high-stakes assessment</p><p
            class="relc__x"
          >
            A model can read a transcript. It cannot tell you whether the person meant it.
          </p></a
        ><a class="relc" href="/blog/signal-vs-noise-technical-hiring"
          ><p class="relc__k">Technical hiring</p><p class="relc__t">Signal versus noise</p><p class="relc__x">
            Most hiring teams spend their week evaluating people who should never have entered the process.
          </p></a
        ><a class="relc" href="/blog/why-tech-hiring-really-takes-so-long"
          ><p class="relc__k">Technical hiring</p><p class="relc__t">Why tech hiring really takes so long</p><p
            class="relc__x"
          >
            A 327-profile search that closed in 22 days once the brief got specific.
          </p></a
        >
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="trust band" id="cta">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sub" style="text-align:center;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center">
          <p class="sub__k">Newsletter in preparation</p>
          <p class="sub__h">Get notified when it is ready.</p>
          <p class="sub__s">Leave your email and we will send one note when the field notes newsletter opens.</p>
          <form
            class="apply__form"
            data-newsletter-form
            action="/.netlify/functions/site-inquiry"
            method="post"
            enctype="multipart/form-data"
            style="display:flex;justify-content:center;width:fit-content;margin-inline:auto"
          >
            <input type="hidden" name="form_type" value="newsletter" /><input
              type="hidden"
              name="form_loaded_at"
            /><input
              type="text"
              name="botcheck"
              tabindex="-1"
              autocomplete="off"
              aria-hidden="true"
              style="position:absolute;left:-9999px"
            />
            <div class="apply__row apply__row--2">
              <input
                class="apply__mail"
                type="email"
                name="email"
                required
                placeholder="your@email.com"
                aria-label="Your email address"
              />
              <button class="apply__go" type="submit" disabled>Notify me<span class="apply__ar">→</span></button>
            </div>
          </form>
          <noscript
            ><p class="apply__fine">
              For updates, email <a href="mailto:hello@tribera.ai?subject=Field%20notes%20notifications"
                >hello@tribera.ai</a
              >.
            </p></noscript
          ><p class="apply__fine" data-newsletter-status aria-live="polite">We never share the list.</p><script
            src="/scripts/newsletter-signup.js"
            defer></script>
          <p class="apply__fine">
            Or if you have a role you’re stuck on —
            <a href="/" style="color:var(--onc);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px">see how one ran</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--w close" id="contact">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="shead" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;text-align:center;justify-items:center;gap:8px">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--red" data-v9-reveal>Two doors</p>
            <h2 class="h2" data-v9-reveal style="max-width:none">Two doors. Same room.</h2>
          </div>
          <p class="dek" data-v9-reveal style="--d:60ms;max-width:none">
            If you have a role you’re stuck on, or you’re the one being interviewed, <b
              >both of those start the same way.</b
            >
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="doors" data-v9-reveal style="--d:110ms">
          <div class="door">
            <p class="door__k">If you’re hiring</p>
            <p class="door__t">Three names you’d take any of.</p>
            <p class="door__d">
              A hundred signals, a senior expert on each shortlisted person, and the reasoning behind every rank.
            </p>
            <p class="door__next"><span>Next</span> A 45-minute intake call. Three names back inside 36 hours.</p>
            <a href="/" class="btn btn--dark">See how one role ran</a>
          </div>
          <div class="door">
            <p class="door__k">If you’re looking</p>
            <p class="door__t">One conversation, then teams come to you.</p>
            <p class="door__d">
              Half an hour with someone who has done your job. Anonymous until you decide otherwise.
            </p>
            <p class="door__next"><span>Next</span> Fifteen minutes to set up, and you keep the score.</p>
            <a href="/candidates" class="btn btn--ghost">Create your profile</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="bbb2" data-v9-reveal><span>Belong</span><i></i><span>Build</span><i></i><span>Become</span></p>
        <p class="bbb2__s" data-v9-reveal>Tribe + Era. A hire only works if both sides were right about each other.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <script>
      /* ══ reading progress ══ */
      (function () {
        const bar = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.reading__f'),
          art = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.art');
        if (!bar || !art) return;
        function tick() {
          if (!art || !bar) return;
          const r = art.getBoundingClientRect(),
            vh = innerHeight;
          const total = r.height - vh * 0.4;
          const done = Math.min(Math.max(-r.top + vh * 0.4, 0), Math.max(total, 1));
          bar.style.width = (done / Math.max(total, 1)) * 100 + '%';
        }
        addEventListener('scroll', tick, { passive: true });
        addEventListener('resize', tick);
        tick();
      })();
    </script>
  </Content>
</V9Layout>
